WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:09.108
The nervous system
is fundamentally electric.

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:10.343
When we move our arm,

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:11.644
it moves because a signal

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:13.179
has been sent to the muscle

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:14.480
that controls it,

00:00:14.513 --> 00:00:16.382
and that message
is made of charged atoms

00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:19.118
moving in and out
of nerve cells.

00:00:19.151 --> 00:00:20.820 align:start size:63% position:38%
It's electricity.

00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:22.522
Now, because the brain
is electric,

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.724
we could also use
electricity to record

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:26.492
what the brain is doing

00:00:26.525 --> 00:00:28.828
or bypass it entirely,

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:31.597
-and control a body.
-[beep]

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.833
That means that
we could use our minds

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:36.302
to move other people's bodies,

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:38.704
restore movement to people
who are paralyzed,

00:00:38.737 --> 00:00:41.441
feel through an artificial hand
as if it was our own,

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:44.744
and even read people's minds.

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:46.345
Shocking!

00:00:46.378 --> 00:00:49.416
[theme music playing]

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.402 align:start position:0%
Even though electricity

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:10.603 size:75% position:38%
wasn't really understood

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.205 align:start position:0% line:0%
until the 1800s,

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.373 align:start position:0% line:0%
its ability
to influence the body

00:01:14.406 --> 00:01:15.708 line:0%
had been known since at least

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:17.276 align:start position:0%
ancient Roman times,

00:01:17.309 --> 00:01:18.744 size:75% position:38%
when respected physician

00:01:18.777 --> 00:01:20.613 align:start position:0%
Scribonius Largus

00:01:20.646 --> 00:01:21.747
<i> wrote about a man</i>

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.316
<i> who accidentally stepped</i>

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:24.784
<i> on an electric fish</i>

00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:27.753
<i> and was suddenly relieved
 of gout pain.</i>

00:01:27.786 --> 00:01:29.455
Scribonius did some experiments

00:01:29.488 --> 00:01:31.757
and found that you could put
an electric fish

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:35.528
<i> on your head
 and relieve headaches.</i>

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:36.896
In 1804,

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:38.498
centuries later,

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:40.800
<i> Italian physicist
 Giovanni Aldini</i>

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.369
<i> discovered he could make
 people's muscles move</i>

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:45.138
<i> with electricity.</i>

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:46.472
He amazed the European world

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:47.707
by using electricity

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.342
to animate the corpse

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:50.910
of an executed criminal.

00:01:50.943 --> 00:01:53.179
<i> The corpse opened its eyes</i>

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:55.515
<i> and even seemed to sit up.</i>

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:59.252
But Aldini wasn't just
trying to shock people.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.420
He was showing the world
that neurons,

00:02:01.453 --> 00:02:02.855
the cells that control both

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:05.191
our thoughts and our movements,

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:07.693
operate through electricity.

00:02:07.726 --> 00:02:11.598
[buzzing]

00:02:17.703 --> 00:02:20.773 size:78% position:39%
Imagine bypassing
a person's brain

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:24.343
so as to control their body.

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:25.778
Now normally
when you move your body,

00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:27.780
your brain sends
electrical signals

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:29.215
through nerves.

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:30.950
But if we could use electrodes

00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:32.318
to send those signals,

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:34.487
we could control
a person's body

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.255
without needing their brain
to be involved.

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:38.291
We could control them
with a remote control,

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.893
and that's what
we are about to do today

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:42.395
with cockroaches.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.737 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
I'm here with Tim Marzullo
from Backyard Brains,

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.273 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
and, Tim, you brought
some cockroaches.

00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:56.576
[Tim] Yes, Michael,
cockroaches use their antenna

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.611
to sense their environment
and move around,

00:02:58.644 --> 00:03:01.214 align:start position:0%
and we're going to try
to trick the cockroach

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.815 size:88% position:44%
and control
its movement

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:04.684 size:88% position:44%
for a number
of minutes.

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:05.952
Trick it, how do we trick it?

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:08.821 align:start position:0%
We are going to do a surgery
on the cockroach

00:03:08.854 --> 00:03:10.656 align:start position:0%
where we're going to hijack
the nervous system

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:12.558
to send electrical impulses
to their antenna.

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:14.293 size:81% position:59%
[Michael] All right,
well, let's get to it.

00:03:14.326 --> 00:03:15.995
-[Tim] Let's do it.
-[Michael] I've brought with me

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:18.698
today a state-of-the-art
cockroach operating room.

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:20.800
Now, how do you do surgery
on a cockroach?

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:23.769 size:84% position:42%
We have a jar of ice water,
and to induce anesthesia

00:03:23.802 --> 00:03:25.605 align:start position:0%
we are going to put
the cockroaches

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:27.840 align:start position:0%
in the ice water
and their nervous system

00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:30.309 align:start position:0%
will stop firing
electrical impulses.

00:03:30.342 --> 00:03:32.311 size:84% position:42%
We'll notice after a minute
or two they'll stop moving.

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:34.380
<i> [Michael] Once the roaches
 were anesthetized,</i>

00:03:34.413 --> 00:03:36.382
<i> they were ready
 for surgery.</i>

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:37.717 align:start position:0%
[Tim] All right,
so then now what we're gonna do

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.452 size:78% position:39%
is attach the electrodes.

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:40.686 align:start position:0%
So here we have
the little connector.

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:42.388 align:start position:0%
It has three wires.
It has a ground wire,

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:43.723 align:start position:0%
and it has a wire
for the left antenna

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.524 align:start position:0%
and the right antenna.

00:03:45.557 --> 00:03:47.526 align:start position:0%
I'm going to put
a little bead of Superglue

00:03:47.559 --> 00:03:49.295 size:88% position:44%
on the cockroach right here,

00:03:49.328 --> 00:03:51.430 align:start position:0%
and then I'm just gonna
stick the electrode

00:03:51.463 --> 00:03:53.966 size:75% position:38%
on the cockroach's head.

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:56.302
<i> [Michael] Next,
 we inserted the ground wire</i>

00:03:56.335 --> 00:03:58.671
<i> into the flight muscle
 of the wing.</i>

00:03:58.704 --> 00:03:59.872
-[Tim] There you go.
-[Michael] Cool.

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:01.440
[Tim] I put the wire
in the back.

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:02.875
[Michael]
<i> The remaining two wires</i>

00:04:02.908 --> 00:04:04.944
<i> get inserted
 into the antenna.</i>

00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:06.679 align:start position:0%
[Tim] Now I'm just
gonna snip the antenna

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:08.314 align:start position:0%
right there and we...

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:09.782 align:start size:88% position:13%
[Michael] And now because
it's a hollow tube...

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:10.983 align:start position:0%
...we can stick
the wire in.

00:04:11.016 --> 00:04:12.885 align:start position:0%
[Tim] So now,
what I'm gonna do

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:15.288 align:start position:0%
is I'm just gonna insert it
into the antenna.

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:17.423
-All right?
-[Michael] Got it, wow.

00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:19.792 align:start position:0%
[Tim] And he's ready
for the left antenna.

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:21.694 align:start position:0%
There we go.
It went right in there.

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:23.029 align:start position:0%
I'll put the cockroach

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.598 size:88% position:44%
right back in the ice water.

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:27.967 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now it's worth pointing out
what this does to them

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.568
and to their quality of life.

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:30.836
[Tim] Yeah, after the surgery

00:04:30.869 --> 00:04:32.038 align:start position:0%
I'm gonna take them back
to their homes,

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:33.306 align:start position:0%
snip the wires,
and these cockroaches

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:34.707 align:start position:0%
will then be retired.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.442 align:start size:88% position:13%
[Michael] And the antenna
will grow back?

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:39.345
[Tim] Yes, and I will return
them to our reproducing colony,

00:04:39.378 --> 00:04:40.646 align:start position:0%
and they'll live happy
cockroach lives

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:42.014 align:start position:0%
after the show.

00:04:42.047 --> 00:04:43.849
-[Michael] Fantastic.
-[Tim] All right.

00:04:43.882 --> 00:04:45.718
-So now we're ready to go.
-[Michael] Wonderful.

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:48.621
<i> Our cyber-roach
 was now neurologically wired</i>

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:50.723
<i> for external control.</i>

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:52.758 align:start size:63% position:38%
[Michael] This is
a state-of-the-art

00:04:52.791 --> 00:04:54.460 align:start size:50% position:50%
roach racetrack.

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:56.829 align:start size:88% position:13%
Tim and I are about
to take some robo-roaches

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:58.097 align:start size:53% position:47%
for a test drive.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:59.398 align:start size:50% position:50%
But first, Tim,

00:04:59.431 --> 00:05:01.567 align:start size:63% position:38%
I wanna see how
an ordinary roach

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.736 align:start size:66% position:34%
moves around
and uses its antenna.

00:05:03.769 --> 00:05:04.904 align:start position:0%
[Tim] So let's just see
where this guy goes.

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:05.971 size:88% position:44%
He wants to explore my hand.

00:05:06.004 --> 00:05:07.506 align:start size:63% position:38%
All right,
and then, oh wow.

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:08.841
-Wow.
-Racin' away.

00:05:08.874 --> 00:05:10.910 align:start position:0%
And you can you see
he's hugging that wall.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.546 size:88% position:44%
You can see he keeps tapping
the wall and moving along.

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:15.014 align:start position:0%
He's not in the middle
of the road,

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:16.649
-he's on the edge of it.
-[Michael] Yeah.

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:18.751
<i> As we know,
 roaches use their antennas</i>

00:05:18.784 --> 00:05:20.586
<i> to explore their surroundings</i>

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:22.988
<i> and locate food and shelter.</i>

00:05:23.021 --> 00:05:25.725
<i> When no walls or obstacles
 were sensed,</i>

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:27.126
<i> the roaches felt free</i>

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:28.894
<i> to move around at will.</i>

00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:30.563
-Hey, guys...
-[Tim] Hey, whoa, whoa.

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:31.664
[Michael] follow the rules.

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:33.466
<i> It was time to test-drive</i>

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.368
<i> our robo-roach.</i>

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:36.969 align:start size:63% position:38%
[Michael] So this
is the battery,

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:38.771 align:start size:66% position:34%
along with
the Bluetooth device.

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.406 align:start position:0%
[Tim] And I'm just gonna plug
them in right now.

00:05:40.439 --> 00:05:42.508 align:start size:75% position:25%
[Michael] We're talking
about actual voltages.

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:44.543 align:start position:0%
[Tim] Yes, because
we are talking to the nerves

00:05:44.576 --> 00:05:46.379 align:start position:0%
in a language that
the nerves understand.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.980
<i> [Michael] Tim has built an app</i>

00:05:48.013 --> 00:05:49.448
<i> that communicates
 to the electrode</i>

00:05:49.481 --> 00:05:50.683
<i> on the roach's back.</i>

00:05:50.716 --> 00:05:51.884
<i> When we swipe right,</i>

00:05:51.917 --> 00:05:53.519
<i> the electrode sends a signal</i>

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:54.920
<i> to the roach's left antenna,</i>

00:05:54.953 --> 00:05:57.523
<i> stimulating the sensation
 of an obstacle</i>

00:05:57.556 --> 00:05:59.692
<i> on its left
 which causes the roach</i>

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:02.094
<i> to move right and vice versa.</i>

00:06:02.127 --> 00:06:03.696 align:start size:75% position:25%
Which way should we go,
left or right?

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:04.864 size:81% position:41%
I think we should go left.

00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:07.166 align:start size:50% position:50%
Left. Whoa.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:08.834
-[Tim] Okay, well, that's...
-[Michael] Doing donuts.

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:10.669 align:start position:0%
All right, that was
a little bit too high.

00:06:10.702 --> 00:06:12.838
[Michael]<i> We adjusted
 the intensity of the signal</i>

00:06:12.871 --> 00:06:15.040
<i> to find the optimal setting.</i>

00:06:15.073 --> 00:06:17.743
All right, now, I'm gonna try
to get him to turn right,

00:06:17.776 --> 00:06:19.078
go back through the factory.

00:06:19.111 --> 00:06:20.613
-[Tim] Oh.
-[Michael] Good job.

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:21.947
-[Tim] Okay, very good.
-[Michael] Good job.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.517
Now he doesn't know
if there's a road,

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.552 align:start size:91% position:9%
so I'm gonna have to tell him
to kind of keep going

00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:28.120
-to the right, good.
-[Tim] Okay, very good.

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:30.189 align:start size:88% position:13%
-[Michael]
 Yeah, good, come here.
-All right, where is he?

00:06:30.222 --> 00:06:31.757 align:start position:0%
Did he just-- he wants to go
to the hot air balloon?

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:33.092 align:start size:75% position:25%
He wants to go
to the hot air balloon,

00:06:33.125 --> 00:06:35.194 align:start size:63% position:38%
but I would rather
he go to work

00:06:35.227 --> 00:06:36.762 align:start size:88% position:13%
at the factory,
so I'm gonna-- I'm gonna...

00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:37.963 align:start position:0%
He's like,
"I'll go back to work."

00:06:37.996 --> 00:06:39.432
[Michael] I'm gonna swipe left.

00:06:39.465 --> 00:06:41.700
-[Tim] Okay.
-There, good. All right, left.

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:43.002
-[Tim] Very good.
-[Michael] Now right.

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:45.037
-[Tim] Ooh. All right.
-[Michael] Good.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:46.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[Michael] All right,
 watch this. And turn right.
-[Tim] Okay.

00:06:47.005 --> 00:06:48.174
[Michael] Good, good, good.
Turn right.

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:49.842 align:start position:0%
[Tim] All right,
turns a little bit.

00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:51.544
-[Michael] Turn right, oh.
-[Tim] Oh, you see

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:53.145
-he adapts over time.
-[Michael] He adapts over time.

00:06:53.178 --> 00:06:54.780 align:start size:66% position:34%
So we are taking away

00:06:54.813 --> 00:06:56.782 align:start size:75% position:25%
their autonomous control
of their body.

00:06:56.815 --> 00:06:58.784 align:start size:63% position:38%
We're just causing
them to think

00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:01.220 align:start size:81% position:19%
they should decide to move
one way or the other.

00:07:01.253 --> 00:07:04.123 align:start position:0%
[Tim] Yeah, some people
used to be sort of scared

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:05.858 align:start position:0%
that we're on
the slippery slope

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:08.227 align:start position:0%
where I'm gonna control
a whole humanity

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.763 align:start position:0%
with my electrical stimulation,
but you can see that

00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:12.498
there's so many other sensory
signals that the cockroach

00:07:12.531 --> 00:07:14.099 align:start position:0%
is receiving,
that we're competing with.

00:07:14.132 --> 00:07:16.769
-So, Tim, this was very cool...
-[Tim] Uh-huh.

00:07:16.802 --> 00:07:18.137
-a little bit weird.
-[Tim] Uh-huh.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:19.705 align:start size:63% position:38%
I didn't feel
totally comfortable

00:07:19.738 --> 00:07:21.674 align:start size:63% position:38%
controlling
a living organism.

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:23.108
-Uh-huh.
-But there's something here

00:07:23.141 --> 00:07:25.077 align:start size:75% position:25%
that's, that's really
important for the future

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:26.946 align:start size:75% position:25%
of understanding brains.

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:28.814 align:start position:0%
[Tim] Sure,
we use this technology

00:07:28.847 --> 00:07:32.184 size:84% position:42%
in biomedical applications,
such as cochlear implants,

00:07:32.217 --> 00:07:34.720 align:start position:0%
and deep brain stimulation
to treat Parkinson's.

00:07:34.753 --> 00:07:37.523 align:start position:0%
But things such as
consciousness and attention

00:07:37.556 --> 00:07:40.526 align:start position:0%
and will are currently
really at the cutting edge

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:41.694 size:78% position:39%
of neuroscience research.

00:07:41.727 --> 00:07:43.128 align:start position:0%
How do you
make decisions?

00:07:43.161 --> 00:07:44.797 align:start position:0%
The wonderful thing
about, uh,

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.099 align:start position:0%
neuroscience is
that it's wide open.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.642
[Michael]
<i> In the very near future,</i>

00:07:56.675 --> 00:07:59.612
<i> cyber cockroaches
 could actually save lives.</i>

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:01.680
<i> Researchers at Texas A&amp;M</i>

00:08:01.713 --> 00:08:04.016
<i> and North Carolina
 State University</i>

00:08:04.049 --> 00:08:06.118 line:0%
<i> are developing
 a groundbreaking use</i>

00:08:06.151 --> 00:08:09.855 line:0%
<i> for robo-roaches in search
 and rescue missions.</i>

00:08:09.888 --> 00:08:11.724 line:0%
<i> Instead of installing wires</i>

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.025 line:0%
<i> in the roach's antennae,</i>

00:08:13.058 --> 00:08:14.860 line:0%
<i> they're implanting receptors</i>

00:08:14.893 --> 00:08:16.996 line:0%
<i> in the bug's
 actual nervous systems</i>

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:19.765 line:0%
<i> to insure
 a longer-term effect.</i>

00:08:19.798 --> 00:08:22.535 line:0%
<i> The plan is to release
 a swarm of robo-roaches</i>

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:23.936
<i> into a disaster area</i>

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:25.604
<i> like a collapsed building.</i>

00:08:25.637 --> 00:08:27.239
<i> As the roaches explore,</i>

00:08:27.272 --> 00:08:30.042 size:88% position:44%
<i> they'll send images
 and data back to a computer</i>

00:08:30.075 --> 00:08:32.144
<i> which creates a detailed 3D map</i>

00:08:32.177 --> 00:08:34.179
<i> of the disaster zone.</i>

00:08:34.212 --> 00:08:36.715 line:0%
<i> If the cyber-bugs stray
 from the search area,</i>

00:08:36.748 --> 00:08:38.584 line:0%
<i> their nerves get stimulated</i>

00:08:38.617 --> 00:08:39.652 line:0%
<i> to steer them back in.</i>

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:41.620
<i> If a roach detects a survivor,</i>

00:08:41.653 --> 00:08:43.222
<i> a signal is sent back</i>

00:08:43.255 --> 00:08:45.691
<i> with the victim's location.</i>

00:08:45.724 --> 00:08:48.227 line:0%
<i> While a real world test
 has not been done yet,</i>

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:50.296 line:0%
<i> scientists hope to do so</i>

00:08:50.329 --> 00:08:53.165 line:0%
<i> within the next 10 years.</i>

00:08:53.198 --> 00:08:54.867
<i> Meanwhile, the same technology</i>

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.869
<i> used to cyber-hack cockroaches</i>

00:08:56.902 --> 00:09:00.540
<i> can also be used on humans.</i>

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:08.047 align:start position:0%
Now, Tim,
when I move my arm,

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.815 align:start position:0%
my brain is sending
electrical signals

00:09:09.848 --> 00:09:12.017 align:start position:0%
to my muscles
and they respond to that,

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:14.253 align:start position:0%
and they contract,
and they release.

00:09:14.286 --> 00:09:17.823 align:start position:0%
But you are about
to use a battery

00:09:17.856 --> 00:09:19.592 align:start position:0%
to electrically
stimulate my muscles.

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:21.193 size:78% position:39%
Sure, sure.

00:09:21.226 --> 00:09:23.295
[Michael]<i> A human's
 nervous system can be hacked</i>

00:09:23.328 --> 00:09:25.664
<i> with no surgery required.</i>

00:09:25.697 --> 00:09:27.933
<i> Tim attached electrodes
 to my forearm,</i>

00:09:27.966 --> 00:09:29.969
<i> which communicated
 via Bluetooth</i>

00:09:30.002 --> 00:09:31.971
<i> to an app on his iPad.</i>

00:09:32.004 --> 00:09:33.839 align:start size:88% position:13%
[Tim] So I'm gonna amplify
the signals

00:09:33.872 --> 00:09:35.174
of your muscle activity.

00:09:35.207 --> 00:09:37.376 align:start size:75% position:25%
So, every time a neuron
talks to the muscle,

00:09:37.409 --> 00:09:39.044 align:start size:75% position:25%
the muscle fires
an electrical impulse

00:09:39.077 --> 00:09:40.713
like the neurons.

00:09:40.746 --> 00:09:42.615 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we can see
these electrical impulses
right here.

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:44.016
So what I want you to do

00:09:44.049 --> 00:09:45.951
-is contract.
-[Michael grunts] Oh, yeah.

00:09:45.984 --> 00:09:47.319 align:start size:75% position:25%
[Tim] All right,
so there you go,
you can see the impulses

00:09:47.352 --> 00:09:49.388
-occurring in your...
-Watch this.

00:09:49.421 --> 00:09:50.856 align:start size:63% position:38%
[Tim] So that whoosh
is the sound

00:09:50.889 --> 00:09:52.291 align:start size:63% position:38%
of many muscle
fibers firing...

00:09:52.324 --> 00:09:54.059
-[Michael] Many, many.
-firing action potentials

00:09:54.092 --> 00:09:56.195 align:start size:91% position:9%
because neurons are synapsing
on those muscles

00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:57.896 size:84% position:58%
when you are deciding
to move your body.

00:09:57.929 --> 00:09:59.865 align:start size:75% position:25%
Let me passively move
your hand.

00:09:59.898 --> 00:10:02.368 align:start position:0%
We don't see that much activity,
maybe some stretch activity,

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:03.902 align:start size:63% position:38%
but now resist me.

00:10:03.935 --> 00:10:05.304
-Now there's no movement...
-[Michael] Wow.

00:10:05.337 --> 00:10:06.872 align:start size:63% position:38%
but we're seeing
electrical activity

00:10:06.905 --> 00:10:08.207 align:start size:63% position:38%
because it's due
to contraction.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:09.308 align:start size:63% position:38%
So what's cool
about this

00:10:09.341 --> 00:10:10.676 align:start size:63% position:38%
is that because
you are making

00:10:10.709 --> 00:10:12.945 align:start size:63% position:38%
voluntary movements
with your brain,

00:10:12.978 --> 00:10:15.881 align:start size:63% position:38%
we can exploit these
muscle contractions

00:10:15.914 --> 00:10:17.216 align:start size:63% position:38%
and use it
to control robotics.

00:10:17.249 --> 00:10:19.051 size:75% position:38%
[Michael] Okay, so, Tim,
this is all really cool,

00:10:19.084 --> 00:10:21.086 align:start position:0%
but the real reason
I invited you here today

00:10:21.119 --> 00:10:22.788 align:start position:0%
and told you to bring
all of this is that

00:10:22.821 --> 00:10:25.658 align:start position:0%
I want to control
another human. Alie?

00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:27.426
-Hi, Michael.
-Thanks for coming.

00:10:27.459 --> 00:10:29.928 align:start position:0%
So, Alie, you might be
a little apprehensive now

00:10:29.961 --> 00:10:31.263 align:start position:0%
after hearing
what I just said, but...

00:10:31.296 --> 00:10:32.931 align:start size:75% position:25%
Yeah, yeah, I heard you.

00:10:32.964 --> 00:10:35.267 align:start position:0%
This is for science,
and it's gonna go both ways.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:37.336 size:81% position:41%
I'm gonna let you control
my arm as well. You ready?

00:10:37.369 --> 00:10:39.104 align:start size:66% position:34%
Okay. Wait, I'm going
first, though?

00:10:39.137 --> 00:10:41.740 align:start position:0%
You're gonna go first,
I think that's important, yes.

00:10:41.773 --> 00:10:43.876
-For...
-For randomization.

00:10:43.909 --> 00:10:45.310
-[Alie] Okay.
-I flipped a coin earlier

00:10:45.343 --> 00:10:47.179
that no one saw,
but it happened.

00:10:47.212 --> 00:10:50.416 line:0%
<i> Alie Ward
 from the</i> Ologies<i> podcast</i>

00:10:50.449 --> 00:10:52.084
<i> was brave enough to let me test</i>

00:10:52.117 --> 00:10:54.353
<i> how the brain sends signals
 to the body,</i>

00:10:54.386 --> 00:10:58.023
<i> in this case
 my brain sending signals</i>

00:10:58.056 --> 00:11:00.259
<i> to move her body.</i>

00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:01.927 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> And we've placed
 a screen between us</i>

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:04.029 size:84% position:42%
<i> so she can't see
 when I make a move</i>

00:11:04.062 --> 00:11:06.432
<i> that sends the signal.</i>

00:11:06.465 --> 00:11:08.033 align:start position:0%
All right, let's do it.
So, Michael.

00:11:08.066 --> 00:11:10.235 align:start size:75% position:25%
Okay. Should I contract
like softly or firmly?

00:11:10.268 --> 00:11:11.837 align:start size:81% position:19%
-Just go all the way
 like that, yes.
-All the way?

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:14.106
-Okay, here we go.
-All right.

00:11:14.139 --> 00:11:16.075
-Did you feel anything?
-Not yet, no.

00:11:16.108 --> 00:11:17.276
-I'm not strong enough.
-Okay, I'm gonna turn it up

00:11:17.309 --> 00:11:18.844 size:88% position:44%
-a little bit, okay?
-[Alie] Okay, yeah.

00:11:18.877 --> 00:11:20.479
It feels like pins and needles
a little bit.

00:11:20.512 --> 00:11:22.014 align:start position:0%
[Tim] Okay. You're at two.
I'm gonna turn up to four, okay?

00:11:22.047 --> 00:11:23.449
-[Alie] All right.
-Okay.

00:11:23.482 --> 00:11:24.917 align:start position:0%
Now contract,
Michael.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.018 align:start size:34% position:66%
Ah!

00:11:27.819 --> 00:11:29.988
That was really, really,
really weird.

00:11:30.021 --> 00:11:32.291
-[Tim] Contract.
-Oh, that's really weird.

00:11:32.324 --> 00:11:34.159
-[Tim] Okay.
-This is really weird.

00:11:34.192 --> 00:11:36.328 align:start size:75% position:25%
Oh, that's-- I'd--
I'm still not over that.

00:11:36.361 --> 00:11:38.497 align:start size:63% position:38%
It feels like, uh,
I'm a marionette

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:40.799 align:start size:75% position:25%
and there's just strings
being tugged.

00:11:40.832 --> 00:11:43.102
[Michael]<i> For the sake
 of science, it was only fair</i>

00:11:43.135 --> 00:11:46.338
<i> that I give Alie a turn
 at the controls.</i>

00:11:46.371 --> 00:11:47.773
<i> But I also wanted to know</i>

00:11:47.806 --> 00:11:49.241
<i> what it was like
 to have someone</i>

00:11:49.274 --> 00:11:51.176
<i> control my movements.</i>

00:11:51.209 --> 00:11:53.445 size:81% position:59%
All right, now while
Alie controls my body,

00:11:53.478 --> 00:11:56.081 align:start size:81% position:19%
I thought it would be nice
to maybe enjoy some hot,

00:11:56.114 --> 00:11:57.816 align:start size:75% position:25%
bright-red tomato soup

00:11:57.849 --> 00:11:59.451
and a nice glass
of grape juice.

00:11:59.484 --> 00:12:02.287
I might even do some writing,
[scoffs] multitasking.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.991 align:start size:75% position:25%
So, um, can we try out
the strength,

00:12:06.024 --> 00:12:07.359 align:start size:63% position:38%
uh, just, like, get
a bit of a sense

00:12:07.392 --> 00:12:08.761
-of what's gonna happen to me?
-All right.

00:12:08.794 --> 00:12:09.995 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, I'll put it
really low at a two.

00:12:10.028 --> 00:12:11.163
-[Michael] Good, yeah.
-[Tim] Okay.

00:12:11.196 --> 00:12:13.165 size:66% position:33%
And, uh, okay,
now contract, Alie.

00:12:13.198 --> 00:12:14.800 align:start position:0%
All right.

00:12:14.833 --> 00:12:16.935
-Ah, okay, yup, I felt it.
-Contract again.

00:12:16.968 --> 00:12:18.137
-I felt...
-Okay. Did you feel that?

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:19.304
-Yes.
-[Tim] All right, now,

00:12:19.337 --> 00:12:20.939 size:88% position:44%
I'm gonna turn it up
to a four.

00:12:20.972 --> 00:12:23.075 align:start size:75% position:25%
Okay.

00:12:23.108 --> 00:12:24.376 size:66% position:33%
All right,
now contract.

00:12:24.409 --> 00:12:27.079
-You ready?
-Yup.

00:12:27.112 --> 00:12:29.882
[laughter]

00:12:29.915 --> 00:12:31.083
Wow.

00:12:31.116 --> 00:12:32.417
That's, um,

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:35.187
that's not me moving my arm.

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:37.089 align:start position:0%
Or the rest of your
entire body responds.

00:12:37.122 --> 00:12:38.957 align:start size:75% position:25%
[Michael] Well-- yeah,
I'm a jumpy person,

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:40.359 size:84% position:58%
so you're gonna get
a lot more out of me.

00:12:40.392 --> 00:12:43.262
-Okay.
-[Michael] Now, please

00:12:43.295 --> 00:12:45.464 align:start size:63% position:38%
don't control me

00:12:45.497 --> 00:12:47.966
while I just take a nice,
relaxing sip of...

00:12:47.999 --> 00:12:51.103 align:start position:0%
[Alie chuckles]

00:12:51.136 --> 00:12:52.938
[Tim] I'll turn it up
to five and a half.

00:12:52.971 --> 00:12:55.541
[Michael] Okay, I'm just
gonna take a nice little,

00:12:55.574 --> 00:12:58.077 align:start size:72% position:28%
uh, taste of this soup.

00:13:00.612 --> 00:13:03.415 align:start position:0%
This is so mean.
I love it. [chuckles]

00:13:03.448 --> 00:13:07.152
The meanest part is that
this is very lukewarm soup.

00:13:07.185 --> 00:13:09.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
All right. Now, just let me
please eat in peace.

00:13:11.423 --> 00:13:13.158
-I'm sorry.
-[Michael] All right,

00:13:13.191 --> 00:13:14.960 align:start size:75% position:25%
I'm gonna take a break
and write something.

00:13:14.993 --> 00:13:16.261 align:start position:0%
I'll leave you alone,
I'll leave you alone.

00:13:16.294 --> 00:13:17.296 align:start size:25% position:75%
Okay.

00:13:21.266 --> 00:13:22.501 align:start size:38% position:63%
[groans]

00:13:22.534 --> 00:13:24.970 align:start size:75% position:25%
I'm try--
I can resist it, but...

00:13:25.003 --> 00:13:27.072
<i> Like the cockroaches,
 my body was learning</i>

00:13:27.105 --> 00:13:29.074
<i> to adapt to the signal.</i>

00:13:30.208 --> 00:13:31.477
To science.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:36.949
That's the beauty
of electrophysiology.

00:13:46.658 --> 00:13:47.993
Of course the fact that our
neurons

00:13:48.026 --> 00:13:49.361
work through electricity

00:13:49.394 --> 00:13:51.930
means that we can also
record from them,

00:13:51.963 --> 00:13:54.399
and that has amazing
medical applications.

00:13:54.432 --> 00:13:57.269 line:0%
The electroencephalogram,
or EEG,

00:13:57.302 --> 00:13:58.937 line:0%
came about in the 1920s,

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:01.039 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> allowing doctors to see
 some of the brain's</i>

00:14:01.072 --> 00:14:03.942 line:0%
<i> electrical signals
 as visible brain waves.</i>

00:14:03.975 --> 00:14:05.410 line:0%
<i> But by the 1980s,</i>

00:14:05.443 --> 00:14:07.646
scientists realized
that for some tasks,

00:14:07.679 --> 00:14:09.882
like the dream of allowing
paralyzed patients

00:14:09.915 --> 00:14:11.316
to move a robotic arm,

00:14:11.349 --> 00:14:13.285
you need a much richer
brain signal.

00:14:13.318 --> 00:14:14.386
<i> That's because moving an arm</i>

00:14:14.419 --> 00:14:16.188
<i> requires a lot of information.</i>

00:14:16.221 --> 00:14:18.223
<i> You need information
 for how to move your upper arm,</i>

00:14:18.256 --> 00:14:19.625
<i> your forearm, your hand,</i>

00:14:19.658 --> 00:14:21.994
<i> your fingers,
 all through 3D space.</i>

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:24.496
EEG just doesn't carry enough
information to do that,

00:14:24.529 --> 00:14:26.431
since the electrical signals
it picks up

00:14:26.464 --> 00:14:28.400
get distorted
after they make their way

00:14:28.433 --> 00:14:30.302
through our skull and skin.

00:14:30.335 --> 00:14:32.905
So, if you wanted to control
a prosthetic limb

00:14:32.938 --> 00:14:35.507
with just your mind,
researchers realized

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:39.044
you'd need to record
directly from the brain.

00:14:39.077 --> 00:14:40.312
And in 2006,

00:14:40.345 --> 00:14:42.447
scientists finally
did just that.

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:44.149
<i> They implanted an electrode</i>

00:14:44.182 --> 00:14:45.984
<i> in the brain
 of a paralyzed human</i>

00:14:46.017 --> 00:14:47.486
<i> for the very first time.</i>

00:14:47.519 --> 00:14:49.288
<i> And since that
 first test patient,</i>

00:14:49.321 --> 00:14:50.923
<i> several paralyzed patients</i>

00:14:50.956 --> 00:14:52.357
<i> have received brain implants</i>

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.426
<i> that allow them
 to control robotic arms</i>

00:14:54.459 --> 00:14:56.061
<i> with their minds.</i>

00:14:56.094 --> 00:14:59.131
<i> As our understanding
 of the brain grows</i>

00:14:59.164 --> 00:15:00.465
<i> and our computers get better</i>

00:15:00.498 --> 00:15:02.601
<i> at decoding signals
 from the brain,</i>

00:15:02.634 --> 00:15:05.938
<i> these patients are gaining
 finer and finer control</i>

00:15:05.971 --> 00:15:08.340
<i> over these robotic arms.</i>

00:15:08.373 --> 00:15:11.343
<i> Recently, science has taken
 a big leap forward</i>

00:15:11.376 --> 00:15:13.211
<i> from allowing
 paralyzed patients</i>

00:15:13.244 --> 00:15:15.414
<i> to control robotic limbs</i>

00:15:15.447 --> 00:15:18.350
<i> to giving them control
 of their own limbs.</i>

00:15:18.383 --> 00:15:21.586
<i> I traveled
 to Ohio State University</i>

00:15:21.619 --> 00:15:24.523
<i> to meet one of those patients,
 Ian Burkhart.</i>

00:15:24.556 --> 00:15:27.159
<i> At 19, while vacationing
 with friends,</i>

00:15:27.192 --> 00:15:29.294
<i> Ian dove into a wave
 at the beach</i>

00:15:29.327 --> 00:15:31.463
<i> and broke his neck
 on the sandy floor,</i>

00:15:31.496 --> 00:15:35.167
<i> leaving him paralyzed
 from the chest down.</i>

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:36.668
[Ian] I knew as soon as I hit

00:15:36.701 --> 00:15:38.103 align:start size:66% position:34%
that I was paralyzed.

00:15:38.136 --> 00:15:40.138 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
And I was floating
face-down in the water

00:15:40.171 --> 00:15:42.007 line:0%
-and could not get up.
-Could you...

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:43.442
[Ian] I couldn't move
anything at all.

00:15:43.475 --> 00:15:45.711
<i> You go from being 19
 and independent,</i>

00:15:45.744 --> 00:15:47.980
<i> living on your own
 in college, to now.</i>

00:15:48.013 --> 00:15:49.681
<i> I need help doing everything</i>

00:15:49.714 --> 00:15:52.617
<i> from eating to going
 to the bathroom,</i>

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:55.187
<i> sitting up,
 getting into my wheelchair.</i>

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:56.555
<i> I can move my shoulders
 pretty well,</i>

00:15:56.588 --> 00:15:57.990 align:start size:63% position:38%
can move my biceps
that allow me

00:15:58.023 --> 00:15:59.124 align:start size:63% position:38%
to move my arms
a little bit,

00:15:59.157 --> 00:16:01.026 align:start size:63% position:38%
but nothing
from my biceps down.

00:16:01.059 --> 00:16:03.395 line:0%
[Michael]<i> The doctors
 at Ohio State had approached</i>

00:16:03.428 --> 00:16:06.498
<i> Ian about a revolutionary
 experimental treatment</i>

00:16:06.531 --> 00:16:08.266
<i> developed by Battelle,</i>

00:16:08.299 --> 00:16:09.501
<i> a research
 and development firm</i>

00:16:09.534 --> 00:16:11.203
<i> using cutting-edge technology</i>

00:16:11.236 --> 00:16:12.604
<i> called NeuroLife.</i>

00:16:12.637 --> 00:16:14.506
<i> They wanted to implant a chip</i>

00:16:14.539 --> 00:16:16.174
<i> in Ian's motor cortex,</i>

00:16:16.207 --> 00:16:18.543
<i> which would allow him
 to move his hand again</i>

00:16:18.576 --> 00:16:21.780
<i> while he was connected
 to a computer in the lab.</i>

00:16:21.813 --> 00:16:24.649
<i> I was invited
 to the Wexner Medical Center</i>

00:16:24.682 --> 00:16:27.319
<i> at Ohio State University
 to meet the researchers</i>

00:16:27.352 --> 00:16:30.122
<i> and witness this cutting-edge
 work in action.</i>

00:16:30.155 --> 00:16:32.791
[Sam] So first we're gonna plug
Ian into the system.

00:16:32.824 --> 00:16:35.193
[Ian] The piece, uh, is
protruding from my skull as...

00:16:35.226 --> 00:16:37.162 align:start size:88% position:13%
-[Michael] Yeah.
-[Ian] ...reference,
 that's the pedestal.

00:16:37.195 --> 00:16:39.164
It's under
a little protective cap,

00:16:39.197 --> 00:16:43.035
um, but it's there 24/7
and it leads to some wires

00:16:43.068 --> 00:16:44.703
that go to the actual
microchip array

00:16:44.736 --> 00:16:47.205
that's on the surface
of my brain.

00:16:47.238 --> 00:16:49.641 align:start position:0%
[Michael] Pushed into
the actual organ of the brain.

00:16:49.674 --> 00:16:51.143 align:start size:50% position:50%
[Ian] Exactly.

00:16:51.176 --> 00:16:52.477 size:88% position:44%
[Michael]
<i> The microchip in Ian's head</i>

00:16:52.510 --> 00:16:53.779
<i> detects the blood flow</i>

00:16:53.812 --> 00:16:56.148
<i> and electrical impulses
 of his brain</i>

00:16:56.181 --> 00:16:58.216
<i> that are associated
 with causing movement.</i>

00:16:58.249 --> 00:16:59.651
[Sam]
So this is the actual chip.

00:16:59.684 --> 00:17:01.153
It sits on the surface
of his brain.

00:17:01.186 --> 00:17:02.487
[Michael] Wow.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:04.189
<i> Ian's brain is connected
 to software</i>

00:17:04.222 --> 00:17:06.258
<i> that reads and analyzes</i>

00:17:06.291 --> 00:17:07.526
<i> his brain activity.</i>

00:17:07.559 --> 00:17:09.828 align:start size:69% position:31%
So this is a live look

00:17:09.861 --> 00:17:11.496 align:start size:69% position:31%
at Ian's motor cortex.

00:17:11.529 --> 00:17:12.664 align:start position:0%
[Marcie] Yes.

00:17:12.697 --> 00:17:15.734 align:start size:75% position:25%
So if you think about
moving your arm,

00:17:15.767 --> 00:17:17.436 size:84% position:58%
we should see more activity

00:17:17.469 --> 00:17:19.604
-or a different pattern?
-[Ian] Yeah.

00:17:19.637 --> 00:17:21.506
[Michael]
<i> At the start of the session,</i>

00:17:21.539 --> 00:17:24.309
<i> in order to calibrate
 the decoder with his brain,</i>

00:17:24.342 --> 00:17:26.111
<i> Ian is prompted
 to think about</i>

00:17:26.144 --> 00:17:28.447
<i> moving his hand
 by watching images</i>

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:30.215
<i> of hand movements.</i>

00:17:30.248 --> 00:17:32.617 align:start position:0%
[Ian] You'll see
my computer prompt me

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:34.853 align:start position:0%
and as it prompts me
to do that,

00:17:34.886 --> 00:17:36.755 align:start position:0%
that's what
I'm thinking about.

00:17:36.788 --> 00:17:38.290 align:start size:50% position:50%
What do you mean
"think about?"

00:17:38.323 --> 00:17:40.292 align:start position:0%
[Ian] Just as much
as I can think about

00:17:40.325 --> 00:17:42.661
-imagining that movement.
-Yeah.

00:17:42.694 --> 00:17:45.831
<i> As Ian's brain thinks
 about moving his hand...</i>

00:17:45.864 --> 00:17:47.766
-[Sam] Okay, Ian, are you ready?
-Yup.

00:17:47.799 --> 00:17:49.501
[Michael]
<i> ...its electrical signals</i>

00:17:49.534 --> 00:17:51.103
<i> are detected by the chip</i>

00:17:51.136 --> 00:17:53.638
<i> and sent to the decoder.</i>

00:17:53.671 --> 00:17:55.107 align:start position:0%
Then we take
that information

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:57.776 align:start position:0% line:0%
and we associate it
with a hand closed.

00:17:57.809 --> 00:18:00.178 align:start position:0% line:0%
So now the decoder
can recognize

00:18:00.211 --> 00:18:02.280 align:start position:0%
if he's thinking
that same thing again.

00:18:02.313 --> 00:18:04.382
[Michael]<i> Next,
 the decoder sends a signal</i>

00:18:04.415 --> 00:18:06.284
<i> to a device that can control</i>

00:18:06.317 --> 00:18:08.120
<i> Ian's muscle movement.</i>

00:18:08.153 --> 00:18:10.222
<i> This special sleeve
 on Ian's arm</i>

00:18:10.255 --> 00:18:12.390
<i> contains a hundred
 and thirty electrodes,</i>

00:18:12.423 --> 00:18:15.127
<i> which stimulate the muscles
 in his forearm</i>

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:17.195
<i> to produce the specific
 movements</i>

00:18:17.228 --> 00:18:19.464
<i> that his brain
 is thinking about,</i>

00:18:19.497 --> 00:18:21.266
<i> such as closing his hand</i>

00:18:21.299 --> 00:18:22.901
<i> or moving his thumb.</i>

00:18:22.934 --> 00:18:26.638
<i> Now it was time to see Ian
 move his paralyzed hand</i>

00:18:26.671 --> 00:18:28.273
<i> with his thoughts.</i>

00:18:28.306 --> 00:18:30.275 align:start position:0%
[Sam] So, Ian, if you're ready
for this next one...

00:18:30.308 --> 00:18:31.877
-[Ian] Yup.
-I'm gonna count you down.

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:34.346 align:start position:0%
Three, two, one.

00:18:40.885 --> 00:18:42.154
[Michael] Whoa.

00:18:42.187 --> 00:18:45.457 align:start position:0% line:0%
[Marcie] So,
that was the decoder.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:46.658 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
Decoding his thoughts?

00:18:46.691 --> 00:18:48.293 line:0%
-Decoding his thoughts.
-[Michael] Wow.

00:18:48.326 --> 00:18:50.529
[Marcie] Ian was in control
of the movements.

00:18:50.562 --> 00:18:52.297
[Michael] Nice work.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:54.232
<i> Already, Ian's achievements</i>

00:18:54.265 --> 00:18:56.868
<i> go far beyond
 closing his hand.</i>

00:18:56.901 --> 00:18:59.204
<i> With the help of this
 cutting-edge technology,</i>

00:18:59.237 --> 00:19:01.339
<i> he can use
 his own paralyzed hand</i>

00:19:01.372 --> 00:19:03.341
<i> to do a number
 of fine motor tasks</i>

00:19:03.374 --> 00:19:06.178
<i> from everyday movements</i>

00:19:06.211 --> 00:19:08.813
<i> to playing Guitar Hero.</i>

00:19:08.846 --> 00:19:10.882
How many other people
are like Ian?

00:19:10.915 --> 00:19:13.285 align:start size:72% position:28%
Currently in the world,

00:19:13.318 --> 00:19:15.520 align:start size:38% position:63%
maybe five?

00:19:15.553 --> 00:19:18.757
[Michael]<i> Ian continues
 to gain more independence</i>

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:21.693
<i> outside the lab, as well.</i>

00:19:21.726 --> 00:19:23.628 align:start position:0%
Do people
ever drive with you

00:19:23.661 --> 00:19:25.630 align:start position:0%
and get nervous like,
"Oh, my gosh,

00:19:25.663 --> 00:19:27.465 align:start position:0%
well, clearly
this is dangerous."

00:19:27.498 --> 00:19:29.367 align:start position:0%
[Ian] I think
if everyone else

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:30.735 align:start position:0%
that was driving
along in the road knew

00:19:30.768 --> 00:19:32.904 size:78% position:39%
I have no use of my hands

00:19:32.937 --> 00:19:34.439 align:start size:75% position:25%
going 70 miles an hour
down the freeway,

00:19:34.472 --> 00:19:35.840 align:start size:75% position:25%
they may not like that.

00:19:35.873 --> 00:19:37.375 align:start position:0%
But they've-- they have
nothing to worry about.

00:19:37.408 --> 00:19:38.843 align:start size:75% position:25%
[Ian] Right, yeah,
and I'm probably safer

00:19:38.876 --> 00:19:40.979 size:81% position:59%
because I can't take
my hands off the wheel

00:19:41.012 --> 00:19:43.348
-to get on my cell phone and...
-Right. Yup.

00:19:43.381 --> 00:19:45.217 align:start position:0%
You're not fiddling
with the radio and...

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:47.252
Yeah, that I shouldn't be
doing while I'm driving.

00:19:47.285 --> 00:19:50.288
<i> [Michael] Ian's progress
 was truly inspiring,</i>

00:19:50.321 --> 00:19:52.524
<i> and this was only
 the beginning for him.</i>

00:19:52.557 --> 00:19:56.361 align:start position:0%
What are your hopes
for the future for yourself?

00:19:56.394 --> 00:19:59.264 align:start size:63% position:38%
[Ian] Hopefully,
I can upgrade

00:19:59.297 --> 00:20:01.733 align:start size:63% position:38%
to the next version
and, you know,

00:20:01.766 --> 00:20:03.001 align:start size:69% position:31%
have more capabilities

00:20:03.034 --> 00:20:05.870 align:start size:75% position:25%
and be able to do things
a lot easier.

00:20:05.903 --> 00:20:08.340
<i> In the long term, I hope
 that this can be a device</i>

00:20:08.373 --> 00:20:10.008
<i> that people just use.</i>

00:20:10.041 --> 00:20:12.244
<i> You have someone
 who has a spinal cord injury</i>

00:20:12.277 --> 00:20:13.578
<i> and they can just continue</i>

00:20:13.611 --> 00:20:15.247
<i> right where they left off.</i>

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.482 align:start size:78% position:22%
I think, you know,
the work that we're doing

00:20:17.515 --> 00:20:20.619 size:81% position:59%
is something that has
never been done before

00:20:20.652 --> 00:20:24.756
and it's really exciting to be
on that cutting edge.

00:20:31.362 --> 00:20:33.431
Restoring movement
is one thing,

00:20:33.464 --> 00:20:35.700
but what about feeling,

00:20:35.733 --> 00:20:37.269 size:75% position:38%
sensation?

00:20:37.302 --> 00:20:39.504
We feel with our skin
because of signals

00:20:39.537 --> 00:20:42.774
sent to the somatosensory
cortex of our brain.

00:20:42.807 --> 00:20:45.677
By putting sensors
in robotic fingers

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:47.445
connected to implants
in a person's

00:20:47.478 --> 00:20:48.947
somatosensory cortex,

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:50.949
researchers
at the University of Pittsburgh

00:20:50.982 --> 00:20:52.884
have enabled Nathan Copeland

00:20:52.917 --> 00:20:57.656
to feel
through a robotic hand.

00:20:57.689 --> 00:20:59.724
<i> Nathan underwent
 brain surgery</i>

00:20:59.757 --> 00:21:01.459
<i> to receive two implants,</i>

00:21:01.492 --> 00:21:02.894
<i> one in his motor cortex</i>

00:21:02.927 --> 00:21:04.763
<i> to control a robotic arm</i>

00:21:04.796 --> 00:21:05.897
<i> and a second implant</i>

00:21:05.930 --> 00:21:08.566
<i> over his somatosensory cortex.</i>

00:21:08.599 --> 00:21:09.768
<i> This second implant</i>

00:21:09.801 --> 00:21:11.536
<i> stimulates his brain</i>

00:21:11.569 --> 00:21:13.571
<i> and the pattern
 of stimulation depends</i>

00:21:13.604 --> 00:21:15.607
<i> on what part
 of the robot hand is touched.</i>

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:18.543
<i> So, if you touch the robot
 on a specific finger,</i>

00:21:18.576 --> 00:21:21.546
<i> then the part of Nathan's
 somatosensory cortex</i>

00:21:21.579 --> 00:21:23.782
<i> that normally responds
 to that finger</i>

00:21:23.815 --> 00:21:25.817
<i> get stimulated.</i>

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:27.919
[Nathan] Pinky.

00:21:27.952 --> 00:21:29.721
Uh, middle.

00:21:29.754 --> 00:21:33.024
[Michael]<i> His brain interprets
 that as touch.</i>

00:21:33.057 --> 00:21:35.894
<i> Nathan and Ian's
 remarkable achievements</i>

00:21:35.927 --> 00:21:37.662
<i> have been aided
 by their ability</i>

00:21:37.695 --> 00:21:39.964
<i> to communicate
 with the researchers involved.</i>

00:21:39.997 --> 00:21:41.833
<i> But what can be done
 for people</i>

00:21:41.866 --> 00:21:44.903
<i> who are not only
 unable to move,</i>

00:21:44.936 --> 00:21:47.372
<i> but also unable to speak?</i>

00:21:47.405 --> 00:21:49.507
<i> Steve Kaplan was an active</i>

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:52.744
<i> 57-year-old computer programmer
 dating Laurie,</i>

00:21:52.777 --> 00:21:55.046
<i> a horse wrangler
 for the rodeo.</i>

00:21:55.079 --> 00:21:57.048
<i> But that all changed
 a year ago</i>

00:21:57.081 --> 00:21:59.017
<i> when he suffered
 a massive stroke</i>

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:01.419
<i> leaving him
 in a vegetative state,</i>

00:22:01.452 --> 00:22:03.655
<i> unable to move or speak.</i>

00:22:03.688 --> 00:22:06.591
<i> Doctors initially declared
 Steve brain dead.</i>

00:22:06.624 --> 00:22:08.526
<i> However, inside his body</i>

00:22:08.559 --> 00:22:09.794
<i> he was fully conscious,</i>

00:22:09.827 --> 00:22:11.930
<i> but unable to tell them so.</i>

00:22:11.963 --> 00:22:13.832
<i> It wasn't
 until a nurse noticed</i>

00:22:13.865 --> 00:22:16.568
<i> that Steve's eye movements
 were intentional</i>

00:22:16.601 --> 00:22:19.704
<i> that he was diagnosed
 with locked-in syndrome,</i>

00:22:19.737 --> 00:22:21.940
<i> a condition which robs
 people of everything</i>

00:22:21.973 --> 00:22:23.675
<i> but their mind.</i>

00:22:23.708 --> 00:22:26.544
<i> While a normal brain
 receives electrical signals</i>

00:22:26.577 --> 00:22:29.013
<i> from the senses and sends
 signals back to them,</i>

00:22:29.046 --> 00:22:31.549
<i> the stroke prevented
 the electrical signals</i>

00:22:31.582 --> 00:22:34.152
<i> from leaving Steve's brain.</i>

00:22:34.185 --> 00:22:35.954
<i> Over the last year,</i>

00:22:35.987 --> 00:22:37.956
<i> with the help
 of physical therapists,</i>

00:22:37.989 --> 00:22:39.824
<i> Steve has regained
 some very minor</i>

00:22:39.857 --> 00:22:42.093
<i> range of motion in his neck.</i>

00:22:42.126 --> 00:22:45.630
<i> I sat down with Steve
 and his now-wife Laurie</i>

00:22:45.663 --> 00:22:47.932
<i> to learn more about
 locked-in syndrome.</i>

00:22:47.965 --> 00:22:50.735 align:start position:0%
Steve, Laurie, hello.

00:22:50.768 --> 00:22:52.904
<i> Although he's still
 unable to speak,</i>

00:22:52.937 --> 00:22:54.906
<i> thanks to new scientific
 developments,</i>

00:22:54.939 --> 00:22:57.175
<i> Steve can communicate
 with the outside world</i>

00:22:57.208 --> 00:22:58.877
<i> using a computer.</i>

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:00.645
[computer male voice]
<i> Hello, Michael.</i>

00:23:00.678 --> 00:23:01.646 align:start position:0%
[Michael]
Nice to meet you, Steve.

00:23:01.679 --> 00:23:02.714 align:start position:0%
And nice
to meet you, Laurie.

00:23:02.747 --> 00:23:03.882 align:start position:0%
First of all,
thank you so much

00:23:03.915 --> 00:23:05.483 size:75% position:38%
for giving us some time.

00:23:05.516 --> 00:23:08.420 size:81% position:41%
Tell me how Steve started
being able to communicate.

00:23:08.453 --> 00:23:10.522
[Laurie] Well, you know,
I asked a lot of questions

00:23:10.555 --> 00:23:11.723
about locked-in syndrome

00:23:11.756 --> 00:23:13.024
when they explained
what it was,

00:23:13.057 --> 00:23:15.026
and there wasn't
a lot of answers.

00:23:15.059 --> 00:23:16.628
-Yeah.
-[Laurie] At the beginning,

00:23:16.661 --> 00:23:19.764
he would blink once for yes
and twice for no.

00:23:19.797 --> 00:23:22.834
<i> And that was the first parts
 of the communication.</i>

00:23:22.867 --> 00:23:24.502
<i> And then they
 gave us a board,</i>

00:23:24.535 --> 00:23:26.204
<i> and I went
 through the ABCs,</i>

00:23:26.237 --> 00:23:27.705
<i> and he would close his eyes</i>

00:23:27.738 --> 00:23:28.807
<i> when I got to what he wanted.</i>

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:29.941
<i> I'd write it down,</i>

00:23:29.974 --> 00:23:31.476
<i> and that's how it began.</i>

00:23:31.509 --> 00:23:33.077
[Michael]<i> As you could imagine,</i>

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:35.647
<i> Laurie and Steve's
 first method of communicating</i>

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:38.883
<i> was a slow
 and painstaking process.</i>

00:23:38.916 --> 00:23:40.785
<i> But just months later,</i>

00:23:40.818 --> 00:23:42.487
<i> an engineer working to help</i>

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:43.688
<i> locked-in patients</i>

00:23:43.721 --> 00:23:44.923
<i> built Steve a machine</i>

00:23:44.956 --> 00:23:46.891
<i> that tracks his eye movements.</i>

00:23:46.924 --> 00:23:49.961
<i> As Steve looks at different
 letters on a screen,</i>

00:23:49.994 --> 00:23:52.497
<i> the machine translates
 where he's looking</i>

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:54.632
<i> into synthesized
 audible speech.</i>

00:23:54.665 --> 00:23:56.501
[computer male voice]
<i> I want to recover.</i>

00:23:56.534 --> 00:23:59.070
[Michael]<i> Although
 there's still a slight delay</i>

00:23:59.103 --> 00:24:01.005
<i> in communication
 as Steve scans</i>

00:24:01.038 --> 00:24:02.674
<i> the letters on the screen,</i>

00:24:02.707 --> 00:24:04.509
<i> this method is much more fluid</i>

00:24:04.542 --> 00:24:06.945
<i> and significantly faster.</i>

00:24:10.114 --> 00:24:12.150 align:start position:0% line:0%
The computer helps me
communicate

00:24:12.517 --> 00:24:14.586 align:start position:0%
[Michael] Yeah.
You can write with your eyes.

00:24:14.619 --> 00:24:15.787
[Laurie]
This thing's amazing.

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:17.989 size:78% position:39%
He can email,
he can text,

00:24:18.022 --> 00:24:19.491
call me
if he needs something.

00:24:19.524 --> 00:24:20.825
It's such a relief.

00:24:20.858 --> 00:24:21.893 align:end size:81% position:81%
[computer male voice]
<i> It does wonders.</i>

00:24:21.926 --> 00:24:23.561 align:start position:0%
[Michael]
It does wonders, yes.

00:24:23.594 --> 00:24:24.762
-Uh-hmm, [chuckles]
-[Michael] And I could only

00:24:24.795 --> 00:24:26.664 align:start position:0%
imagine how emotional
it would have been

00:24:26.697 --> 00:24:29.100 align:start position:0%
to you to be able to look
and write a thing,

00:24:29.133 --> 00:24:30.568 size:75% position:38%
and then have it spoken.

00:24:30.601 --> 00:24:33.004 align:start position:0%
And, Laurie,
for you to hear

00:24:33.037 --> 00:24:35.874 align:start position:0%
words coming from his mind,
from his heart,

00:24:35.907 --> 00:24:37.575 align:start position:0%
especially
for the first time

00:24:37.608 --> 00:24:39.143
-in a long time.
-[Laurie] Yes.

00:24:39.176 --> 00:24:41.613 align:start position:0%
What was the first thing
you said, Steve?

00:24:47.585 --> 00:24:49.554
[computer male voice]<i> The first
 message was happy birthday.</i>

00:24:49.587 --> 00:24:51.823
[laughter]

00:24:51.856 --> 00:24:53.024 size:75% position:38%
What a birthday present.

00:24:53.057 --> 00:24:54.826
[Laurie laughs]
It really was.

00:24:54.859 --> 00:24:57.128
[Michael]<i> Steve's progress
 is remarkable,</i>

00:24:57.161 --> 00:24:58.863
<i> and it would be
 difficult to deny</i>

00:24:58.896 --> 00:25:01.165
<i> the connection between
 his new ability to communicate</i>

00:25:01.198 --> 00:25:02.901
<i> and connect
 with the outside world</i>

00:25:02.934 --> 00:25:04.602
<i> with the incredible advances</i>

00:25:04.635 --> 00:25:06.938
<i> he's made physically
 over the last year.</i>

00:25:06.971 --> 00:25:09.007
-Look up at the ceiling.
-[Michael] Yeah.

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:11.976
-Look down at your thumbs.
-[clears throat]

00:25:12.009 --> 00:25:13.912
Look over at me,
your beautiful wife.

00:25:13.945 --> 00:25:15.680
[laughter]

00:25:15.713 --> 00:25:17.849
[Michael]<i> Laurie and Steve
 hope that one day</i>

00:25:17.882 --> 00:25:19.317
<i> Steve will make
 a full recovery</i>

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:20.886
<i> from locked-in syndrome.</i>

00:25:26.657 --> 00:25:29.594
Steve's eye-tracking device
is electric,

00:25:29.627 --> 00:25:30.995
and so is his brain.

00:25:31.028 --> 00:25:32.597
They're connected by the fact

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:34.599
that Steve can move his eyes.

00:25:34.632 --> 00:25:36.167
But some locked-in patients

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:38.937
can't even control
their eye movements.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:40.171
They have what is known

00:25:40.204 --> 00:25:43.741
as completely
locked-in syndrome.

00:25:43.774 --> 00:25:46.210
For these patients,
the eye-tracking technology

00:25:46.243 --> 00:25:49.247
that helped Steve communicate
is useless.

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:51.749 line:0%
<i> But researchers in Europe
 recently found a way</i>

00:25:51.782 --> 00:25:53.952 line:0%
<i> of allowing completely
 locked-in patients</i>

00:25:53.985 --> 00:25:57.355
<i> to communicate
 using just their thoughts.</i>

00:25:57.388 --> 00:25:59.624
<i> The researchers combined EEG</i>

00:25:59.657 --> 00:26:02.293
<i> with near-infrared
 spectroscopy,</i>

00:26:02.326 --> 00:26:03.895
<i> which can non-invasively</i>

00:26:03.928 --> 00:26:06.064
<i> measure blood flow
 in the brain.</i>

00:26:06.097 --> 00:26:07.198
The patients were asked

00:26:07.231 --> 00:26:08.900
simple yes or no questions

00:26:08.933 --> 00:26:10.868
that the researchers
knew the answers to,

00:26:10.901 --> 00:26:13.738
which allowed them
to train a machine to recognize

00:26:13.771 --> 00:26:15.907
which brain signals meant yes

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:18.242
and which brain signals
meant no.

00:26:18.275 --> 00:26:20.311
The researchers
then asked the patients

00:26:20.344 --> 00:26:21.813
a new set of questions

00:26:21.846 --> 00:26:22.947
they knew the answer to,

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.382
and using just their thoughts,

00:26:24.415 --> 00:26:26.117
<i> the patients were able to give</i>

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:29.754
<i> the correct answers
 to 70% of the questions.</i>

00:26:29.787 --> 00:26:32.256
Now, giving just
yes or no answers

00:26:32.289 --> 00:26:33.391
may be limited,

00:26:33.424 --> 00:26:35.126
but this technology is new.

00:26:35.159 --> 00:26:37.895
All of this happened
just this year.

00:26:37.928 --> 00:26:39.797
<i> These rapid advancements,</i>

00:26:39.830 --> 00:26:43.201
<i> thanks to the devotion
 and effort of researchers</i>

00:26:43.234 --> 00:26:44.869
<i> and subjects around the world,</i>

00:26:44.902 --> 00:26:46.704
<i> are slowly but surely</i>

00:26:46.737 --> 00:26:49.707
<i> chipping away
 at the mysteries of the mind.</i>

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:51.242
The better
we understand the mind,

00:26:51.275 --> 00:26:52.777
the better
we'll be able to help

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:54.412
the millions of people
who struggle to do

00:26:54.445 --> 00:26:56.781
what they want
because of psychological

00:26:56.814 --> 00:26:58.416
or neurological conditions,

00:26:58.449 --> 00:27:02.987
and ultimately the better
we'll understand ourselves.

00:27:03.020 --> 00:27:04.856
And as always,

00:27:04.889 --> 00:27:06.024
thanks for watching.

00:27:08.192 --> 00:27:11.129 line:0%
[theme music playing]

